<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.6.2: Describing How Two Variables Are Related</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00794</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.6.2: Describing How Two Variables Are Related</md:title>
    <md:uuid>197463f8-05af-4627-b0f0-c76afae514ca</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Each of the scatter plots shows a strong relationship. Write a sentence or two
        describing how you think the variables are related.</para>
      <!-- Begin Question1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">1. During the month of April, Elena keeps track of the number of inches of rain, or
            precipitation, recorded for the day and the percentage of people who come to school with
            rain jackets.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing a positive correlation between precipitation in inches (x-axis) and percentage of people wearing jackets (y-axis), with a trend line indicating increased jacket use as precipitation rises.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_03_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00005">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">When there is more rain, it is more likely people will wear jackets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- End Question 1-->
      <!-- Begin Question2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">2. A school book club has a list of 100 books for its members to read. They keep
            track of the number of pages in the books the members read from the list and the amount
            of time it took to read the book.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing the relationship between time in hours (x-axis) and number of pages (y-axis). A blue trend line indicates a strong positive correlation as time increases. Data points cluster closely to the line.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_03_02.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Longer books will take more time to read.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- End Question 2-->
      <!-- Begin Question 3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">3. Number of tickets left for holiday parties at a venue and noise level at the
            party</para>
          <para id="para-00013">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing noise level in decibels (y-axis) versus number of tickets left (x-axis), with a downward trend line indicating noise decreases as more tickets remain.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_03_03.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00015">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00016">When more tickets are left, the party will have fewer people to have loud
            conversations, so it will be quieter.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- End Question 3-->
      <!-- Begin Question 4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">4. The height and score on a test of vocabulary for several children ages 6 to 13</para>
          <para id="para-00018">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing noise level in decibels (y-axis) versus number of tickets left (x-axis), with a downward trend line indicating noise decreases as more tickets remain.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_03_04.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00020">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Children who are taller are usually older, so they have had more time to be in
            school and learn more vocabulary.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- End Question 4-->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
          <media alt="Illustration of a woman in overalls and a headscarf pushing a wheelbarrow with a potted plant and gardening tools outdoors, surrounded by plants and clouds.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_03_05.png" width="300"/>
        </media>
        
        <para id="para-00022">One of the most powerful tools you can give a farmer to help improve their crop is statistics. Statistics can help farmers at every stage. For example, seeds are expensive, and if you
        plant too many strawberries in a market where there are hundreds of strawberry farmers, you might not sell enough to make a profit. With statistical models, farmers can use demand to precisely calculate the proper amount of crops to plant.</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00002">
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
        <para id="para-00023"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_3_6_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
  </section>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00005"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Causal Relationships </title>
        <para id="para-00025">A panel is rating different kinds of potato chips. The scatter plot below shows the
          relationship between their average rating and the price of the chips, along with the line
          of best fit. How could the variables be related?</para>

        <media alt="Graph of a scatter plot and its line of best fit on a coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the price of the chips in dollars and the y-axis represents the average number of points the chips were rated.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_05_01.jpg"/>
        </media>

        <para id="para-00026">As the price of a bag of chips in dollars increases, the average rating of the bag of
          chips in points increases.</para>
        <para id="para-00027">Does this represent a causal relationship or simply an association?</para>
        <para id="para-00028">In this situation, the independent variable, price, does not seem to cause the rating
          a person gives the chips. While there appears to be a positive association, there are
          other factors that may cause the ratings for a bag of chips to go up. For instance,
          advertising on tv or social media might cause a particular kind of chip to increase in
          ratings without a change in their price.</para>

        <note class="try numbered-exercises" id="note-00003">
        <title>Try It: Describing a Relationship Using a Scatter Plot</title>
        <!-- Begin Question1-->
          <media alt="Graph of a scatter plot and its line of best fit on a coordinate plane that shows the relationship between the car age in years, which is represented on the x-axis, and the car's mileage in thousands of miles, which is graphed on the y-axis.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_08_05_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00029">The scatter plot shows a strong relationship. How could the variables be related?</para>
            <para id="para-00030"> </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00032">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. As a car gets older, it is expected to
              have been driven more and have more miles on it as the years go by.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
